His snout is grey and his ears are notched from old fights, and he has the
gravel voice of a soldier long retired. He has seen enough war, he growls,
and these days he pays travellers instead of robbing them. On your first
visit he grunts and tosses you 30 rupees without looking up from his
whittling. Walk in again and you get a shrug and a hard look, and nothing
else at all.
