away. Ash still powders its top. Through it lies a neat square room with
two fires, a broom, and a wooden plate nailed beside the frame where twenty
rupees are laid down. The stair beyond the door goes up to the field.
~
0 AD 11
D4
~
burn door repair entrance~
4 0 30224
S
#30706
The River-Damp Repair Shop~
Grey paste coats the damp steps where ash from the burned riverbank bush
met the river mist, and the mended door at their foot is swollen with river
mist, its new boards paler than the old. The room inside smells of wet
sawdust. Two fires burn on a raised hearth to keep the floor dry, and a
ledger lies open on a stool, every line of it reading twenty. Steps climb
back to the thicket.
~
0 AD 11
D4
~
burn door repair entrance~
4 0 30226
S
#30707
Seaside Mended Door~
The boom of the sea carries faintly through the rock into this room, whose
blasted entrance now wears a mended door made of salvaged ship planks,
still crusted with salt. Rubble has been stacked into a low bench along one
wall. Two fires burn in the middle of the floor, and a scallop shell set on
the bench holds the twenty-rupee charge of whoever came before. A stair
leads up to the shore path.
~
0 AD 11
D4
~
bomb door repair entrance~
4 0 30213
S
#30708
Behind the Gouged Cliff~
Light that once showed between the cracks now leaks around the edges of a
hastily mended door, its planks fitted unevenly into the gouged cliff.
Chips of rock from the blast crunch underfoot. Two fires burn low near a
stack of spare boards, and an upturned helmet by the fires serves as the
collection box for the twenty rupees every visitor owes for the damage.
Stairs lead up to the cliffs.
~
0 AD 11
D4
~
bomb door repair entrance~
4 0 30310
S
#30710
The Gorge Gaming Den~
Broken gorge rock has been kicked into a corner to make room for the game.
Three rupee markers sit in a straight row on the swept floor, painted alike
so that nothing tells them apart, and two fires throw their glow across
them. The wind howling through the gorge outside can be heard faintly.
Scuffs before the markers show where players have paid their ten. The way
out climbs up through the broken wall.
~
0 AD 11
D4
~
bomb gamble entrance~
4 0 30296
S
#30711
The Wager Room~
Every time a rock bounces down the gully outside, the three rupee markers
laid on the floor of this room jump a little and settle again. The clinking
heard through the cracks is plain now: stacks of coin are piled near the
two fires, waiting to be won or swallowed at ten a try. A line scratched
before the markers shows where a player stands to choose. Gravel from the
blast covers the stair up to the gully.
~
0 AD 11
D4
~
bomb gamble entrance~
4 0 30302
S
#30712
Dice Rattle Cavern~
Behind the cracked crag face, the rattle heard outside turns out to be a
cup of dice left on a ledge, a toy beside the real game. Three rupee
markers lie spaced evenly across the floor between two fires, each one a
gamble that might fatten a ten-rupee stake or empty a purse. Hard-packed
dirt before them is worn smooth by nervous feet. The stair leads up through
the blasted rock to the crags.
~
0 AD 11
D4
~
bomb gamble entrance~
4 0 30207
S
#30713
Sandblown Gambling Cave~
Dune sand keeps finding its way in through the bombed entrance, and it has
drifted around the three rupee markers set out on the floor so that only
their tops show. Two fires burn behind them. The stacked coins heard from
outside sit in towers of different heights, winnings and losses side by
side. Ten small notches cut into the floor mark the price of a turn. A
sandy stair rises to the dunes.
~
0 AD 11
D4
~
bomb gamble entrance~
4 0 30212
S
#30714
The Cliffside Game Parlour~
Morning light from the cliff shelf reaches only a little way into this cave
before the glow of two fires takes over. Three rupee markers lie in a row
on a strip of faded red carpet, and a battered cup sits beside a heap of
coins where the laughter and rattling came from. The floor before the
carpet is scuffed by countless visitors who stopped to try their luck at
ten a turn. Stairs lead up to the shelf.
~
0 AD 11
D4
~
open gamble entrance~
0 0 30311
S
#30720
The Western Crossway~
The scraped earth under the moved boulder gives onto a long hall, cool and
echoing, its far side pierced by three stairways set at even distances,
west, centre and east. Each descends into darkness that smells faintly of
somewhere else, grass in one, sand in another, water in the third. Two
fires burn at the hall's midpoint. The way back climbs up to the boulder
and the trampled grass.
~
0 AD 11
D4
~
bracelet warp hall entrance~
4 0 30283
S
#30721
Hall Beneath the Meadow Stone~
A draught pours up from below the moved stone and runs the length of this
hall, setting the two fires leaning. Three stairways open in the floor
along the far side, one to the west, one in the centre and one to the east,
each with its steps worn by travellers who chose and were carried far away.
Turf roots hang from the ceiling near the entrance. Stairs climb up into
the boulder meadow.
~
0 AD 11
D4
~
bracelet warp hall entrance~
4 0 30257
S
#30722
The Southern Warp Gallery~
Fresh-turned earth from beneath the great stone has spilled down onto the
first steps of this gallery. It is long and plain, built of dressed grey
blocks, and three stairways break its floor at the west end, the centre and
the east end, each one a road to a distant part of Hyrule. A pair of fires
lights the gallery from its centre. The way out is up to the stone field.
~
0 AD 11
D4
~
bracelet warp hall entrance~
4 0 30209
S
#30723
Frost-Rimed Crossroads Hall~
High in the mountains, the hall under the heavy boulder is colder than its
kin, frost glittering on the stone near the entrance. Three stairways open
in a row along its length, west, centre and east, and wind breathes out of
each one carrying the scent of some lowland far away. Two fires burn
steadily despite the chill, their smoke drawn down the stairways. The way
out climbs up to the high meadow.
~
0 AD 11
D4
~
bracelet warp hall entrance~
4 0 30309
S
#30750
The Lost Woods~
Every trunk here looks like every other, and the paths twist so strangely
that a traveller can walk out one side of the grove and find the same trees
waiting. Someone has cut a line into the bark of the oldest oak: NORTH,
WEST, SOUTH, WEST. A cave mouth above the trail breathes a bitter smell of
simmering herbs, and near the path a lone dead bush stands brittle enough
to catch from a single spark.
~
